WEBVTT

00:01.040 --> 00:03.040
[theme song playing]

00:07.600 --> 00:09.600
[indistinct chattering]

00:13.360 --> 00:15.400
[laughing]

00:15.480 --> 00:17.000
God, I'm getting big now.

00:40.120 --> 00:44.680
- So, how was London?
- It's crackling with revolution.

00:44.840 --> 00:46.920
The Poplar docks are on strike.

00:47.040 --> 00:49.480
Did our friends give us
what we asked for?

00:52.080 --> 00:54.640
- How much?
- Two hundred pounds.

00:54.720 --> 00:56.400
Who did you meet?

00:56.480 --> 00:58.600
An attache from
the Russian embassy.

00:59.120 --> 01:00.880
In a Chinese restaurant.

01:01.840 --> 01:05.840
Our revolution is international.
And it grows by the day.

01:14.280 --> 01:16.400
[chattering]

01:21.480 --> 01:23.720
Jeremiah, Jeremiah...
What do you see?

01:24.640 --> 01:27.320
Your sister and Freddie
got back this morning.

01:27.400 --> 01:31.240
I tried following them,
but Freddie's so good at getting away.

01:31.800 --> 01:33.080
He's like a fish.

01:33.680 --> 01:35.680
Right, well, keep fishing, eh?

01:55.960 --> 01:58.520
We wait until the boy's in position.

02:01.680 --> 02:05.640
- [Scudboat] Last bets now!
- Don't spend your rent money, Charlie!

02:06.600 --> 02:09.800
That's your starters, gentlemen.
No more bets.

02:11.680 --> 02:13.960
- I was here on time.
- Finished.

02:14.040 --> 02:17.520
- I got a tip off, I need this bet.
- The race has started.

02:17.600 --> 02:18.640
- Please.
- No!

02:18.720 --> 02:20.400
[sighs] Fine.

02:20.480 --> 02:24.040
- Get rid of him.
- [Arthur] We said no, now get out!

02:24.120 --> 02:26.720
[man] All right.
All right, I'm off. I'm off.

02:42.640 --> 02:45.520
Night, lads.
See you tomorrow.

03:05.800 --> 03:08.080
- It's a good day.
- Where's John?

03:08.160 --> 03:09.840
John is in the Garrison.

03:09.920 --> 03:12.760
He says he wants a meeting
about a family matter.

03:13.360 --> 03:17.800
After he's said his piece he'll come back
and take his place with Scudboat.

03:17.880 --> 03:20.400
Scudboat? John will
be here in ten minutes.

03:20.480 --> 03:22.600
- All right.
- Five.

03:51.240 --> 03:52.280
Wait.

04:00.360 --> 04:01.960
Good boy.

04:18.600 --> 04:19.800
[creaking]

04:19.920 --> 04:21.000
John?

04:30.360 --> 04:31.480
[all shouting]

04:31.640 --> 04:33.440
Put that down!
Put that down!

04:35.440 --> 04:36.840
This is for Cheltenham.

04:37.760 --> 04:39.800
We're just taking
back what's ours.

04:40.600 --> 04:43.120
There's more money here.
Search everywhere.

04:48.640 --> 04:51.240
All right John, there's only one man...
No!

04:51.320 --> 04:53.640
There's only one man
guarding the house.

04:54.200 --> 04:55.720
What's troubling you?

04:56.000 --> 04:57.600
[clearing throat]

04:57.680 --> 05:01.600
Polly, you know what it's been like.
Since Martha died.

05:01.680 --> 05:03.280
God takes the best first.

05:03.640 --> 05:07.960
Truth is, my kids have been
running bloody rings around me.

05:09.240 --> 05:12.040
Running barefoot with
the dogs until all hours.

05:13.640 --> 05:16.920
Pol, give him ten bob for some shoes.
Is that it, John?

05:17.000 --> 05:19.880
Tommy, it would be better
to do this without you.

05:20.680 --> 05:22.040
Now what's your point?

05:23.880 --> 05:26.320
What the kids need is a mother.

05:29.480 --> 05:31.560
So that's why I'm getting married.

05:32.920 --> 05:35.960
Does this poor girl know
you're going to marry her,

05:36.040 --> 05:39.200
or are you going to spring
it on her all of a sudden?

05:39.480 --> 05:41.760
I already proposed
and she said, "Yes."

05:41.840 --> 05:45.280
I think there's a shell
about to land and go bang.

05:48.600 --> 05:51.120
It's, er...
It's Lizzie Stark.

05:51.200 --> 05:53.840
[laughing]

05:55.440 --> 05:57.640
John, Lizzie Stark
is a strong woman,

05:57.720 --> 06:01.240
and I'm sure she provides
a fine service for her customers.

06:01.320 --> 06:06.760
I won't hear the word.
Understand? Do not use that word.

06:06.840 --> 06:08.440
What word is that, John?

06:09.040 --> 06:10.720
You know what word that is.

06:10.800 --> 06:14.360
- Everybody bloody knows.
- Everybody can go to hell.

06:15.320 --> 06:16.640
Whore. That word?

06:16.720 --> 06:19.760
Or prostitute?
How about that one?

06:19.840 --> 06:24.560
Right. I want it known.
If anyone calls her a whore again,

06:24.640 --> 06:28.000
I will push the barrel of my revolver
down their throats,

06:28.080 --> 06:30.760
and blow the word
back down into their hearts.

06:30.840 --> 06:34.280
Men and their cocks
never cease to amaze me.

06:34.360 --> 06:37.480
John, Lizzie Stark never
did a day's work vertical...

06:37.560 --> 06:40.440
She's changed.
All right. People change.

06:40.520 --> 06:45.760
- Like with religion.
- Lizzie Stark has got religion, eh?

06:45.840 --> 06:48.320
No! No, she doesn't have religion. But--

06:48.960 --> 06:50.320
But she loves me.

06:51.040 --> 06:55.600
Now listen, Tommy.
I won't do it without your blessing.

06:57.080 --> 07:02.640
But of all the people in the world,
I want you to see it...

07:03.920 --> 07:06.760
- As brave.
- Oh, it's brave all right.

07:06.840 --> 07:09.920
[Polly] Brave is going where no man
has gone before.

07:10.520 --> 07:14.000
And with Lizzy Stark, John
that's not what you'll be doing.

07:14.080 --> 07:16.560
Listen, Tommy.
Welcome her to the family.

07:17.360 --> 07:19.560
As someone who's had a hard life.

07:20.960 --> 07:22.960
All right? Because I need someone.

07:24.960 --> 07:26.480
The kids need someone.

07:27.240 --> 07:28.240
[door opens]

07:28.320 --> 07:31.240
- Tommy! We've been done over!
- What?

07:36.480 --> 07:38.720
Jesus Christ!

07:38.800 --> 07:41.000
What the bloody hell happened here?

07:41.080 --> 07:43.960
[Scudboat] The Lees. All of them.

07:44.040 --> 07:47.360
Cousins, nephews,
even their bastards...

07:47.440 --> 07:50.960
[Polly] They've taken anything they can
lay their hands on.

07:53.040 --> 07:54.120
Four cash boxes.

07:56.320 --> 07:57.680
They left these.

07:57.760 --> 08:00.720
Wire cutters.
Why would they leave that?

08:00.800 --> 08:01.840
Nobody move.

08:04.080 --> 08:06.520
I think our friends
are playing the game.

08:06.600 --> 08:07.640
What game?

08:07.720 --> 08:10.320
Aunt Pol.
Don't touch anything.

08:12.560 --> 08:14.120
Erasmus Lee was in France.

08:15.480 --> 08:16.520
Shit...

08:20.960 --> 08:23.520
Whenever we gave up
ground to the Germans,

08:25.040 --> 08:29.160
we'd leave behind booby traps,
set up with wires.

08:29.240 --> 08:32.400
We'd leave wire cutters
as part of the joke.

08:33.640 --> 08:36.000
Somewhere in here
there's a hand grenade.

08:36.080 --> 08:37.520
Holy Jesus.

08:39.360 --> 08:41.080
Attached to a wire.

08:43.159 --> 08:47.320
Don't move any chairs
or open any doors.

08:50.920 --> 08:53.040
Go easy, John Boy. Go easy.

08:53.120 --> 08:54.400
No.

08:54.480 --> 08:56.920
Boys, no. It's not in here.

08:58.720 --> 09:01.440
If it was in here,
it would have blown by now.

09:01.520 --> 09:05.040
It was my name on that
bullet Erasmus sent.

09:06.320 --> 09:08.120
He's set up a trap all right.

09:11.120 --> 09:13.040
But he's set it up just for me.

09:26.200 --> 09:27.240
Finn.

09:29.200 --> 09:31.200
Finn stay exactly where you are.

09:32.680 --> 09:34.320
I was pretending I was you.

09:37.120 --> 09:39.280
Which door did you open to come in?

09:39.360 --> 09:41.400
I didn't. I climbed in.

09:42.880 --> 09:48.080
I want you to climb out exactly the
same way you climbed in, okay?

09:49.440 --> 09:50.600
No. Finn.

09:52.960 --> 09:54.040
Clear!

09:58.880 --> 10:00.360
[man] Are you all right?

10:00.440 --> 10:01.760
It could've killed us.

10:02.440 --> 10:04.160
[both panting]

10:17.120 --> 10:21.520
That's why you should never
pretend to be me, okay?

10:21.640 --> 10:22.760
Okay?

10:42.560 --> 10:43.760
[whistles]

10:59.880 --> 11:01.920
I got you ten minutes with her.

11:02.000 --> 11:03.280
[woman shouting]

11:03.920 --> 11:07.880
You could say thank you.
It's easier to see the Pope these days.

11:39.200 --> 11:41.440
Put your hand on the Bible.

11:41.520 --> 11:42.720
I don't believe.

11:44.320 --> 11:46.280
I know I didn't come here to lie.

11:47.560 --> 11:49.560
This war is cutting us all off.

11:50.680 --> 11:53.240
A boy almost got killed.

11:53.560 --> 11:55.000
[laughing]

11:56.160 --> 11:57.280
A child.

11:57.360 --> 11:58.880
You're all children.

11:59.440 --> 12:01.120
I say enough.

12:01.200 --> 12:04.040
Your boys tried to kill me
and it didn't work.

12:07.680 --> 12:10.400
I come here with a proposition.

12:14.480 --> 12:16.320
I thought he was your ally.

12:16.840 --> 12:19.000
I plan to betray him.

12:19.400 --> 12:21.800
No wonder you won't touch a Bible.

12:21.880 --> 12:25.400
- I have ambitions.
- You want to play a switch.

12:25.480 --> 12:27.800
- I need your boys.
- For what?

12:27.880 --> 12:31.960
Kimber's not the brains.
There's a gadze who runs the races.

12:32.040 --> 12:36.320
I'm collecting smart people.
But I need strong men too.

12:36.400 --> 12:38.920
Now your boys should know this.

12:39.000 --> 12:42.280
We get the winner in one
of every three races before

12:42.360 --> 12:45.600
the race even starts.
No need for chalkers or rafflers.

12:45.680 --> 12:47.120
I'm talking certainties.

12:47.200 --> 12:50.000
You're saying you're
going to do someone down,

12:50.080 --> 12:52.560
and in the same breath
ask me to trust you.

12:54.800 --> 12:57.480
On my mother's side, we are kin.

13:00.160 --> 13:03.760
Let us talk family business.

13:12.880 --> 13:14.440
Ada, can you do my back?

13:17.720 --> 13:18.760
Ada?

13:20.840 --> 13:22.760
I know what was in the envelope.

13:23.680 --> 13:25.960
I looked. Money.

13:26.040 --> 13:29.240
That bloke we met in London
you said was a Frenchman,

13:29.320 --> 13:31.920
he was Russian.
I'm not an idiot, Freddie.

13:32.000 --> 13:37.200
How is it you take all the risks,
and Stanley Chapman gets the money?

13:37.280 --> 13:41.120
- It's money for the cause.
- You're blind.

13:41.200 --> 13:44.200
I'd be if your brothers had anything
to do with it.

13:44.280 --> 13:46.760
This isn't about them.
This is about you.

13:46.840 --> 13:49.800
And me. And this.

13:53.040 --> 13:55.000
You're a dreamer, Freddie.

13:56.640 --> 13:59.200
You give money away
while we rot in here.

14:00.840 --> 14:03.200
And you talk about revolution.

14:04.000 --> 14:05.240
I need to know.

14:09.240 --> 14:11.480
Who are you loyal to, Freddie?

14:14.720 --> 14:16.880
I said, who are you loyal to?

14:36.120 --> 14:37.880
[door opens]

14:55.440 --> 14:57.680
Did you have something
to do with this?

14:59.440 --> 15:01.800
I asked an acquaintance
for an address.

15:01.880 --> 15:04.760
She said she would only
give it to me anonymously.

15:04.840 --> 15:06.880
She is afraid of the consequences.

15:08.480 --> 15:09.760
Whose address is it?

15:10.760 --> 15:13.000
Tommy, I'd like to suggest a strategy.

15:16.960 --> 15:18.440
[car approaching]

15:34.080 --> 15:37.280
Your message said,
you have an address for me.

15:42.200 --> 15:43.720
Anonymous tip off.

15:45.120 --> 15:47.080
The address of Stanley Chapman.

15:48.880 --> 15:53.120
- You promised me Freddie Thorne.
- This is instead of Freddie Thorne.

15:53.800 --> 15:54.880
No deal.

15:54.960 --> 15:58.960
Inspector, Stanley Chapman is a
bigger fish than Freddie Thorne.

16:01.680 --> 16:05.080
He is currently holding
200 pounds in cash.

16:05.160 --> 16:08.600
Given to the Communist party
by the Russian Government.

16:10.480 --> 16:14.280
That's right, Chapman
has snow on his boots,

16:14.360 --> 16:16.080
and all you need is a shovel.

16:17.240 --> 16:19.560
If he talks, you'll have proof.

16:20.920 --> 16:22.760
You might even get that medal.

16:24.480 --> 16:28.640
Now, before I give you the address,

16:30.040 --> 16:34.960
I want your word,
that you will let Freddie Thorne

16:35.040 --> 16:37.600
and my sister leave the city.

16:41.200 --> 16:43.400
Very well. You have my word.

16:48.840 --> 16:53.080
Our little truce is proving quite
productive for both of us, Inspector.

16:54.760 --> 16:59.880
I get the information
and protection and you...

17:01.640 --> 17:02.840
Well...

17:04.200 --> 17:05.800
You get Bolsheviks.

17:10.520 --> 17:12.359
But on a more pressing matter,

17:14.960 --> 17:18.880
I'm afraid Mr Churchill
is becoming impatient.

17:20.599 --> 17:27.560
And I fear that if you don't get back
those stolen weapons soon,

17:27.640 --> 17:29.640
I will be replaced.

17:29.720 --> 17:32.400
It would be the finish of me.
That's for sure.

17:32.480 --> 17:36.360
When my business with Kimber is done,
the guns will be returned.

17:36.440 --> 17:40.040
- That was the deal.
- Then I am in your hands. Completely.

17:41.760 --> 17:43.440
You hold all the cards.

17:44.520 --> 17:48.840
But I hope to God,
that my dismissal doesn't come

17:48.920 --> 17:51.880
before your decision
to hand back those guns.

17:52.560 --> 17:54.040
I say this for your sake.

17:54.120 --> 17:59.840
Because, if I were to be fired
and it were your fault,

18:02.040 --> 18:04.720
I would do things
that would shame the devil.

18:05.520 --> 18:06.920
[laughs]

18:07.000 --> 18:09.640
My fury is a thing to behold.

18:11.480 --> 18:15.960
On my last day in power for example,
I would see to it

18:16.960 --> 18:19.760
that you and your scum brothers
have your heads,

18:19.840 --> 18:22.640
stoved in with mallets and spades.

18:24.520 --> 18:27.080
And your sister too.

18:27.160 --> 18:31.320
The baby inside her would
be of no consequence to me.

18:31.400 --> 18:37.320
The only one to be spared would be
your little brother, Finn.

18:37.440 --> 18:41.680
He would however be
lifted as a juvenile,

18:41.760 --> 18:45.600
and dumped into that
part of the adult prison,

18:45.680 --> 18:49.760
where men have the most appetite
for boys like him.

18:51.360 --> 18:54.960
That would be a dark day indeed,
Mr. Shelby.

18:56.240 --> 18:59.560
If my dismissal comes
before your decision.

19:00.520 --> 19:01.880
Do you understand?

19:04.440 --> 19:06.160
Know this...

19:08.480 --> 19:09.880
The clock is ticking.

19:58.760 --> 20:00.360
Stanley, it's the police.

20:01.400 --> 20:02.840
Stanley, get here.

20:06.600 --> 20:08.200
[Stanley] You bastards!

20:08.920 --> 20:11.560
[Stanley screaming]

20:17.320 --> 20:18.720
Look what we found.

20:20.200 --> 20:22.840
Stanley Chapman does have snow
on his boots.

20:32.840 --> 20:34.120
Mr. Chapman...

20:36.040 --> 20:37.640
You are fucked!

20:47.720 --> 20:48.960
[door opens]

20:52.240 --> 20:55.680
[Moss] He still refuses
to say where it came from.

20:57.640 --> 21:03.040
I'm sure with a little persuasion,
he'll tell us everything he knows.

21:03.120 --> 21:06.240
Including the whereabouts
of Freddie Thorne.

21:07.160 --> 21:10.520
- Sir?
- Your face is a picture.

21:10.600 --> 21:12.760
I thought you had done a deal.

21:12.840 --> 21:15.720
- Is that what you thought?
- You gave your word.

21:17.240 --> 21:18.880
My word?

21:22.080 --> 21:25.800
Do people still talk about such things
in this idiotic century?

21:25.880 --> 21:28.160
My word to whom?

21:29.440 --> 21:30.920
To a Peaky Blinder?

21:32.720 --> 21:36.920
Now, interrogate Mr. Chapman
until he tells us,

21:37.040 --> 21:39.720
where we can find Freddie Thorne
and his wife.

21:40.440 --> 21:43.480
The source of the money
is of secondary importance.

21:45.880 --> 21:49.360
You think my campaign against Shelby
has become personal?

21:50.200 --> 21:51.840
Correct.

21:51.920 --> 21:54.560
'Spot on', as they
say in London society.

21:55.800 --> 22:00.200
Now, go and interrogate Mr Chapman,

22:00.280 --> 22:03.120
and do not make the mistake of
being too gentle.

22:04.600 --> 22:05.800
Right, sir.

22:07.480 --> 22:08.560
[door opens]

22:10.040 --> 22:11.120
[door closes]

22:37.920 --> 22:39.320
Today's her birthday.

22:42.120 --> 22:44.280
I know you never miss it.

22:44.360 --> 22:46.400
Then I'm lucky you're not a copper.

22:47.040 --> 22:49.720
- What do you want?
- I came to warn you.

22:51.280 --> 22:52.960
They lifted Stanley Chapman.

22:53.760 --> 22:57.240
How do you know?
The police don't spill that information.

22:57.320 --> 22:59.680
It was me and Tommy who tipped them off.

22:59.760 --> 23:02.400
In return for safe passage
for you and Ada,

23:02.480 --> 23:04.760
Tommy gave them Stanley and the money.

23:04.840 --> 23:06.560
Money? What money?

23:09.840 --> 23:12.880
- Who told you about the money?
- Who do you think?

23:14.520 --> 23:17.480
It was Ada's idea.
That's how desperate she is

23:17.560 --> 23:19.840
to get out of that rat hole you're in.

23:22.960 --> 23:26.160
She didn't want to be here
when you found out.

23:26.240 --> 23:29.720
Sometimes the women have to take over.
Like in the war.

23:29.800 --> 23:31.320
You fucking Shelbys--

23:31.400 --> 23:33.640
Don't swear over your mother's grave.

23:34.880 --> 23:38.400
You play your tune and expect
the whole world to dance to it.

23:38.480 --> 23:40.280
You don't have time for this.

23:41.560 --> 23:43.560
You did a deal? For me?

23:43.640 --> 23:45.520
Don't flatter yourself. For Ada.

23:45.600 --> 23:48.640
And you think this copper
will keep his word.

23:48.720 --> 23:51.640
If he doesn't, Chapman will give you up.

23:51.720 --> 23:54.440
You'll still have to leave town.
Same result.

23:55.640 --> 23:57.960
- So neat.
- So leave.

23:58.040 --> 24:00.720
Except there's one thing
that you got wrong.

24:00.800 --> 24:04.520
He won't be able to give up my address.
He doesn't know it.

24:04.600 --> 24:06.160
That's how it works.

24:06.240 --> 24:08.520
None of us know each
other's addresses.

24:09.520 --> 24:11.520
So you wasted your fucking time.

24:12.840 --> 24:17.240
They'll keep beating him and beating him
for information he doesn't have.

24:17.320 --> 24:20.880
So all you've done is sign
the death warrant of a good man.

24:22.520 --> 24:23.760
So you won't leave.

24:23.840 --> 24:27.440
No! I won't fucking leave!
If you want me out of Birmingham,

24:27.520 --> 24:30.040
it'll have to be in a wooden box.

24:35.280 --> 24:39.120
You lay a hand on our Ada,
I'll put you in a wooden box myself.

24:49.960 --> 24:52.280
You raised a stubborn one there, Irene.

25:19.520 --> 25:23.520
[Moss] You told me to carry on.
I wanted to stop.

25:23.600 --> 25:25.600
He had some kind of seizure.

25:30.760 --> 25:32.960
[Campbell sighs]
So you killed him.

25:34.640 --> 25:36.240
Did he give you an address?

25:36.320 --> 25:37.240
[sighs]

25:37.320 --> 25:40.440
What's the matter with you?
He fell down some stairs.

25:40.560 --> 25:42.400
This is not bloody Belfast!

25:42.480 --> 25:46.000
Not yet! But if men like him get
their way, it soon will be.

25:46.080 --> 25:50.760
So find some stairs, throw him down,
and call the coroner.

25:52.480 --> 25:54.240
Use Grayson.

25:54.320 --> 25:57.480
If he has any awkward questions,
ask about the welfare

25:57.560 --> 26:01.280
of his mistress in Salteley.
That will shut him up.

26:07.080 --> 26:10.480
[Kimber] To think I used to
live in a shit hole like this.

26:10.560 --> 26:12.120
Bloody animals.

26:12.200 --> 26:15.400
But the Shelby's are doing
an excellent job for us.

26:16.520 --> 26:18.000
We haven't lost a penny

26:18.080 --> 26:20.880
to rafflers or chalkers
in eight race meetings.

26:20.960 --> 26:24.360
- The bookies are purring.
- So we throw the dog a bone.

26:25.400 --> 26:26.680
I think so.

26:33.360 --> 26:36.760
Mr Kimber. Mr Roberts.
Come and have a look around.

26:44.120 --> 26:45.320
After you.

26:45.400 --> 26:47.960
[Scudboat] You can feed half of Birmingham

26:48.040 --> 26:50.920
with what you've had for lunch.
Get back to work.

26:54.240 --> 26:56.560
We heard the Lees had turned you over.

26:56.640 --> 26:59.160
You shouldn't listen to gossip, Mr Kimber.

26:59.240 --> 27:01.440
[Scudboat] Come on boys. Chop Chop.

27:01.880 --> 27:03.120
Business is good.

27:04.880 --> 27:08.840
Since you know which horse is going to win
before you set the odds.

27:08.920 --> 27:11.440
Your information is very much appreciated.

27:13.440 --> 27:14.840
So, where are they?

27:16.320 --> 27:18.920
John, Lovelock, Scudboat. In here.

27:24.400 --> 27:26.000
This is my team.

27:26.080 --> 27:29.000
They'll take up their pitch
at your convenience.

27:30.040 --> 27:32.400
John is the book.
Scudboat the bag man.

27:32.480 --> 27:35.120
And of course we bring
our own protection.

27:37.720 --> 27:42.120
Warwick. Next Saturday.
At least 50 yards from the beer tent.

28:08.560 --> 28:10.080
Gentlemen...

28:10.160 --> 28:11.440
[Polly clears throat]

28:11.520 --> 28:12.640
...and Lady.

28:13.840 --> 28:18.160
I have in my hand a legal betting license.

28:18.240 --> 28:20.120
Issued by the board of control.

28:21.760 --> 28:27.720
The Shelby family has its
first legal racetrack pitch.

28:27.800 --> 28:29.520
[all cheering]

28:37.520 --> 28:40.000
Congratulations, there you go.

28:43.800 --> 28:46.480
Thirty. Forty. Fifty.

28:53.760 --> 28:56.600
These cigarettes have a
strange smell, Arthur.

28:56.680 --> 28:59.320
They smell like rotting water.

28:59.400 --> 29:01.840
And look. Rats have got at some of them.

29:03.320 --> 29:06.600
- They're stolen, aren't they?
- Don't ask.

29:06.680 --> 29:09.800
- You keep them on a boat.
- What do you care?

29:09.880 --> 29:12.040
Can you check my adding up, please?

29:17.280 --> 29:19.960
You should make a
new start with this place.

29:20.040 --> 29:23.800
Do it properly.
These cigarettes are not fit to sell.

29:23.880 --> 29:25.200
Smells like Gallipoli.

29:25.280 --> 29:27.720
You should find a new
place to store them.

29:27.800 --> 29:30.480
It has to be far away from coppers.

29:30.560 --> 29:33.640
- But not rats?
- All the wharfs have rats, Grace.

29:35.400 --> 29:38.720
- What's wrong with a dry warehouse?
- Tommy's orders.

29:40.480 --> 29:41.920
What orders?

29:42.000 --> 29:45.040
Always keep Contraband
near to petrol boat moorings.

29:45.960 --> 29:47.440
Don't boats get searched?

29:47.520 --> 29:52.240
We moor them at junctions,
so there's more than one way out.

29:52.320 --> 29:56.240
No locks within a mile
so we can move stuff fast.

29:56.960 --> 30:00.040
Your brother doesn't obey the law,
but he has rules.

30:00.160 --> 30:01.680
He's a very precise man.

30:02.200 --> 30:04.960
- Is my adding up right?
- It is now.

30:07.520 --> 30:10.080
[Campbell] One. Two.

30:10.680 --> 30:13.240
Three.And four.

30:14.560 --> 30:16.280
We'll search them one by one.

30:22.880 --> 30:26.360
Please Lord,
let that which I seek be found here.

30:42.560 --> 30:46.600
Not so much as a bullet, sir.
Just more cigarettes and whisky.

30:56.080 --> 30:59.200
[Thomas] Arthur tells me
you've been asking questions.

31:00.520 --> 31:02.960
About how we run our business.

31:03.120 --> 31:06.040
And how we get our booze
and where we keep it.

31:06.120 --> 31:07.600
I'm just trying to help.

31:10.680 --> 31:12.520
Let's you and me go for a walk.

31:13.440 --> 31:15.720
- Where to?
- Come on.

31:25.280 --> 31:27.000
Why here?

31:27.080 --> 31:28.960
[door closes]

31:29.040 --> 31:31.400
You're a good Catholic girl, aren't you?

31:33.520 --> 31:34.960
Yes.

31:35.040 --> 31:38.280
Well, then you know it's here
people come to confess.

31:39.240 --> 31:40.680
After you.

31:51.880 --> 31:53.360
Well, here it is, Grace.

31:55.400 --> 31:56.440
I'll confess.

31:58.720 --> 32:00.240
I need someone.

32:02.120 --> 32:04.960
Kimber has an adviser
by the name of Roberts.

32:05.040 --> 32:07.960
He talks well.
He keeps the accounts.

32:08.520 --> 32:10.840
He runs the legal side of the business.

32:11.880 --> 32:13.480
And you need a Roberts.

32:14.680 --> 32:16.880
Arthur tells me you have ideas.

32:16.960 --> 32:19.080
I'm not an accountant.
Nor a lawyer.

32:19.160 --> 32:20.240
No.

32:20.880 --> 32:22.680
But you have something I need.

32:23.920 --> 32:24.960
Class.

32:26.800 --> 32:29.840
I need someone who looks right
at the big meetings.

32:29.920 --> 32:31.440
Epsom, Ascot.

32:31.520 --> 32:32.960
Is this a job interview?

32:33.040 --> 32:35.320
Arthur says you're good with numbers.

32:35.400 --> 32:38.240
Well, that's relative.
He is quite poor.

32:38.600 --> 32:41.760
- You keep the books in order.
- They were chaotic.

32:42.520 --> 32:44.120
But you are a liar.

32:49.440 --> 32:54.240
No Catholic girl would enter a church
and forget to make the sign of the cross.

32:55.920 --> 32:57.360
You are very perceptive.

32:57.440 --> 33:00.040
You lied about the pub
you used worked in.

33:00.880 --> 33:02.920
Now I find out you are Protestant.

33:05.440 --> 33:07.560
- Do you care?
- No.

33:08.440 --> 33:09.560
I lied to fit in.

33:10.600 --> 33:13.680
You pull a pint like someone
who's thinking about it.

33:13.760 --> 33:16.680
This is not an interview,
it is an interrogation.

33:16.760 --> 33:17.960
Sit down.

33:23.840 --> 33:25.280
Look, Grace...

33:26.880 --> 33:29.440
You washed up in a place
you don't belong.

33:29.520 --> 33:30.760
For whatever reason.

33:32.240 --> 33:35.000
- My good fortune.
- And perhaps mine.

33:41.240 --> 33:43.720
You know that most of
what I do is illegal?

33:43.800 --> 33:45.400
I'm not blind.

33:45.480 --> 33:47.800
You'd still be willing to work for me?

33:50.360 --> 33:52.080
Are you offering me the job?

33:52.800 --> 33:53.800
[scoffs]

33:56.520 --> 33:57.560
Then I accept.

33:59.400 --> 34:01.720
There's something else you should know.

34:03.080 --> 34:06.560
A very important detail about
my reasons for employing you.

34:27.159 --> 34:28.600
You disappoint me.

34:32.080 --> 34:33.440
Do you resign?

34:35.800 --> 34:36.840
No.

34:38.080 --> 34:40.760
My appetite for the work
has only increased.

34:47.239 --> 34:49.080
Tomorrow I'll show you around.

35:01.960 --> 35:03.000
[Thomas] Lizzie.

35:03.760 --> 35:04.880
Hello, Lizzie.

35:06.720 --> 35:08.560
That bag looks heavy, jump in.

35:09.200 --> 35:12.160
- Kids' teas.
- You like kids?

35:12.240 --> 35:15.320
- Yeah.
- Good. John's got four of them.

35:16.800 --> 35:20.000
I'm not going to try to talk
you out of it.

35:32.640 --> 35:34.040
[engine stops]

35:36.720 --> 35:38.080
I just want to talk.

35:38.800 --> 35:40.200
You're not against us?

35:40.840 --> 35:42.120
John's his own man.

35:43.320 --> 35:45.080
And you are your own woman.

35:49.000 --> 35:52.720
Now, Lizzie, since I came back
from France,

35:52.800 --> 35:54.880
I've come to you on many occasions.

35:56.560 --> 35:59.680
- Tommy, you didn't tell him.
- No, I didn't tell him.

36:00.640 --> 36:02.480
Just like you didn't tell him.

36:03.800 --> 36:06.200
Now why didn't you tell him, Lizzie?

36:06.280 --> 36:09.920
Why didn't you tell him that
you'd been serving his brother

36:10.000 --> 36:11.520
for the past two years?

36:12.680 --> 36:14.440
Because the past is the past.

36:16.200 --> 36:18.640
I don't want to lose him.
He's a good man.

36:19.560 --> 36:23.280
That's the answer I was hoping you'd give.
The past is the past.

36:23.360 --> 36:25.520
You see our John says you've changed.

36:25.600 --> 36:28.440
And I believe him.
And that's good.

36:28.520 --> 36:29.920
Change is good.

36:31.720 --> 36:33.520
These are new times I'm told.

36:37.400 --> 36:39.280
I wish you both every happiness.

36:40.520 --> 36:45.400
And I want you to see that
as my wedding gift to you.

36:46.560 --> 36:49.440
And our farewell to pleasures gone by.

37:02.320 --> 37:05.840
You mean...
One last time?

37:08.120 --> 37:10.320
One last time.
You and me.

37:13.600 --> 37:15.480
It's eight bloody pounds.

37:35.360 --> 37:36.840
So where shall we go?

37:39.840 --> 37:41.840
Tommy? Shall we go to my lodging?

37:45.360 --> 37:47.280
So the past is not the past.

37:50.240 --> 37:52.720
You can keep the money.
Get out of the car.

37:52.840 --> 37:55.120
- Tommy, please.
- Get out of the car.

37:55.200 --> 37:56.960
I love him, Tommy. Really.

37:58.360 --> 37:59.400
Really.

38:07.240 --> 38:09.200
John will make his own decision.

38:10.600 --> 38:12.400
But he will have the facts.

38:14.920 --> 38:17.480
Your brother is ten times the man you are!

38:28.880 --> 38:31.440
[sighs] Of that I have no doubt.

38:50.760 --> 38:53.320
He is obviously not stupid.

38:53.400 --> 38:55.960
All we found was stolen
tobacco and whisky.

38:56.040 --> 38:57.480
Which you left in place.

38:57.560 --> 39:00.560
Of course. I would never endanger you.

39:05.920 --> 39:07.560
You are doing well, Grace.

39:09.320 --> 39:11.840
I've been working on Arthur.
He is easier.

39:11.920 --> 39:14.640
- Than Thomas.
- Yes.

39:14.720 --> 39:15.920
Less intelligent.

39:17.040 --> 39:18.520
Yes.

39:18.600 --> 39:21.040
Is that a word you
would use about Thomas?

39:21.120 --> 39:22.160
It's your word.

39:24.680 --> 39:26.920
He has promoted me.

39:27.000 --> 39:30.000
He wants me to be
his bookkeeper and secretary.

39:31.720 --> 39:35.120
A cutthroat gangster with a secretary.

39:35.200 --> 39:38.600
The pretensions of these hoodlums
are quite breathtaking.

39:39.240 --> 39:42.600
- Are they not?
- Yes. Quite breathtaking.

39:45.920 --> 39:49.000
He has obviously fallen
quite heavily for you.

39:49.080 --> 39:51.000
I thought you would be pleased.

39:52.240 --> 39:54.520
Remember who you're dealing with here.

39:54.600 --> 39:57.640
A man who cuts off ears
and cuts out tongues.

39:58.240 --> 40:00.080
I know what he is, Sir.

40:02.240 --> 40:07.200
The difficulty with undercover work,
Grace, is to remember what you are.

40:08.520 --> 40:11.040
I come here with good news and I get this.

40:14.880 --> 40:15.920
Grace.

40:33.680 --> 40:35.320
Tommy.

40:35.400 --> 40:38.480
I need a favor.
I want to borrow the car.

40:38.600 --> 40:41.600
I want to take Lizzie to the country
with the kids.

40:41.680 --> 40:44.280
We're going to celebrate
getting the license.

40:44.360 --> 40:45.640
Not a problem, John.

40:46.840 --> 40:48.200
I'll need the keys.

40:50.640 --> 40:52.560
Look, John, you're my brother.

40:54.600 --> 40:56.840
There's something I have to tell you.

40:58.160 --> 41:03.600
Yesterday, on the front seat of that car,
I offered Lizzie some money.

41:05.000 --> 41:08.160
And John? She said, "Yes."

41:10.360 --> 41:13.520
Now that's a fact.
You do with it what you want.

41:14.480 --> 41:17.880
Take the keys.
Take Lizzie to the country.

41:17.960 --> 41:20.240
Marry her if you want.

41:22.000 --> 41:23.480
But you have to know.

41:24.960 --> 41:26.080
She said, "Yes."

42:00.120 --> 42:01.280
[slams door]

42:09.680 --> 42:11.240
Get another glass.

42:11.320 --> 42:12.560
[clears throat]

42:21.240 --> 42:23.440
You make a toast?

42:23.520 --> 42:26.640
I'm Irish, I can make a million toasts.

42:26.720 --> 42:31.040
May you be in heaven a full half hour
before the devil knows you're dead.

42:37.400 --> 42:38.960
What are we celebrating?

42:39.040 --> 42:41.840
Contract of employment.

42:43.320 --> 42:46.200
Bookkeeper.
Shelby Brothers Limited.

42:46.280 --> 42:49.040
I don't like that word 'limited'.

42:49.120 --> 42:51.600
To be respectable, you have to be limited.

42:51.680 --> 42:53.080
That's what worries me.

42:54.720 --> 42:56.360
I had the phone put in.

42:58.280 --> 42:59.320
It's in the back.

43:01.560 --> 43:05.560
If we knew someone else who had a phone,
we could call them.

43:07.520 --> 43:09.320
And since we are celebrating,

43:10.600 --> 43:13.760
I had this delivered from
Rackhams department store.

43:17.200 --> 43:18.920
Aren't you going to open it?

43:20.280 --> 43:23.760
No. Save it. For a special occasion.

43:25.680 --> 43:30.760
Right, your first job for the company.
I want you to get that to my sister.

43:30.840 --> 43:32.040
You don't see her?

43:32.120 --> 43:34.720
No one does.
She is hiding from me.

43:36.600 --> 43:40.280
I am told she goes to a
bathhouse on Montague Street.

43:40.360 --> 43:42.400
On woman only days.

43:42.480 --> 43:45.680
She goes in disguise,
so I need to get someone inside.

43:45.760 --> 43:47.120
What am I delivering?

43:49.480 --> 43:52.120
It's an invitation
to a family occasion.

43:52.200 --> 43:55.760
I want her there.
So tell her there will be a truce.

43:55.840 --> 43:57.760
Am I delivering bait for a trap?

43:58.680 --> 44:01.880
If you check that contract,
you'll find it doesn't say

44:01.960 --> 44:05.400
anything about asking questions.
Give her the invitation.

44:06.880 --> 44:09.800
And put that thing
in the cupboard, until I say.

44:20.520 --> 44:21.680
[door opens]

44:49.680 --> 44:50.840
[uncocks gun]

44:51.240 --> 44:52.520
What the hell, John?

44:52.600 --> 44:55.320
I couldn't get it lit.
Couldn't even do that.

44:55.400 --> 44:57.640
Why would you want
to be smoking that?

44:57.720 --> 45:00.240
Same reason as you.
Pain in the head.

45:11.360 --> 45:14.920
I spoke to Lizzie.
I told her what you told me.

45:17.640 --> 45:20.120
She said, "Your brother is a dirty liar."

45:21.560 --> 45:24.400
But then I spoke to her sister
and her cousin.

45:24.480 --> 45:26.240
Bought them a couple drinks.

45:27.200 --> 45:29.440
"Just a few of regulars", they said.

45:30.240 --> 45:31.400
"That's all."

45:33.240 --> 45:37.360
"To keep the wolf from the door,
she still sees a couple of regulars."

45:38.840 --> 45:41.240
[sniffs] You must think I'm an idiot.

45:51.080 --> 45:53.640
I think you're
the first Shelby in history,

45:54.400 --> 45:56.760
to have a legal license for anything.

45:56.840 --> 45:57.840
[sighs]

45:59.360 --> 46:04.920
What would our granddad say, eh?
He'd be turning in his grave.

46:05.000 --> 46:10.520
"Honest bloody money? Eh?
In this house? In here?"

46:10.600 --> 46:13.360
You always used to do voices
when we were kids.

46:16.800 --> 46:18.720
Well, we're not kids now, John.

46:21.640 --> 46:24.680
But we still have to look out
for each other, right?

46:26.800 --> 46:28.040
Yeah.

46:29.720 --> 46:32.960
Yeah. Come on. Go home.

46:33.960 --> 46:38.320
Get some sleep.
We've got a big day tomorrow.

46:38.600 --> 46:39.680
We have?

46:41.440 --> 46:44.840
Tomorrow we finish the war with the Lees
once and for all.

46:46.520 --> 46:48.760
- Since when?
- Since just now.

46:50.000 --> 46:51.400
Ten o'clock tomorrow.

46:54.160 --> 46:55.640
Be ready for anything.

47:04.040 --> 47:05.600
Out you get boys.

47:08.520 --> 47:09.880
[Thomas] Ready boys?

47:11.040 --> 47:13.480
- John, ready?
- Yeah, I'm fine.

47:13.560 --> 47:14.600
Have a drink.

47:23.000 --> 47:26.680
What? Why are you all
staring at me for? What?

47:26.760 --> 47:28.360
Good. Let's go.

47:30.360 --> 47:31.640
What?

47:42.120 --> 47:45.080
Tommy, what's your plan?
We are in shotgun range.

47:49.160 --> 47:53.440
John, before we go into battle...

47:53.520 --> 47:55.840
There's something you're going to need.

47:57.680 --> 47:59.640
What are you bloody doing, Tommy?

48:02.080 --> 48:04.080
Smile, John, it's a wedding.

48:04.160 --> 48:05.440
Whose bloody wedding?

48:05.520 --> 48:08.120
Now if we'd told you,
you wouldn't have come.

48:08.200 --> 48:11.600
There's a girl from the Lee family
who's going a bit wild.

48:11.680 --> 48:13.560
And she needs marrying her off.

48:13.640 --> 48:16.120
- Ah, fuck!
- John boy, John, boy!

48:16.200 --> 48:17.280
John!

48:17.360 --> 48:19.440
You have no bloody right, Tommy!

48:19.520 --> 48:21.600
Listen to me. Listen to me.

48:23.160 --> 48:26.240
A girl who needs a husband.
A man who needs a wife.

48:27.720 --> 48:32.720
Tom, I'm not bloody marrying
some fucking mushroom picker...

48:33.120 --> 48:35.320
[shushing] John boy, come on. Listen.

48:36.600 --> 48:39.080
I have already betrothed you.

48:39.160 --> 48:43.480
If you back out now, there's gonna be
a mighty war breaking out here.

48:43.560 --> 48:45.320
It's gonna make the Somme...

48:45.400 --> 48:47.920
It'll make the Somme look
like a tea party.

48:48.000 --> 48:52.480
But if you marry her,
our family and the Lee family

48:52.560 --> 48:55.520
will be united forever.
And this war will be over.

48:55.600 --> 48:57.880
Now, it's up to you John.

48:57.960 --> 49:01.080
War or peace.

49:04.480 --> 49:05.720
Let go of me.

49:19.080 --> 49:20.400
Right.

49:20.480 --> 49:23.720
You should see the size of her dowry.

49:23.800 --> 49:25.560
Her what?

49:25.640 --> 49:27.360
Her dad is giving you a car.

49:34.600 --> 49:35.600
[clears throat]

49:36.640 --> 49:37.760
Will he do?

49:44.280 --> 49:45.320
He'll do.

49:45.400 --> 49:46.920
[all cheering]

49:53.800 --> 49:54.840
Here she is.

49:54.920 --> 49:57.000
[John] She'd better be under 50.

49:57.080 --> 50:00.400
Come here. Go on.

50:08.440 --> 50:11.640
[Johnny Dogs] We're here today
to join in matrimony,

50:11.720 --> 50:14.480
this man and this woman.

50:16.040 --> 50:17.760
You look well.

50:17.840 --> 50:20.280
...and harmony and togetherness.

50:22.600 --> 50:25.440
Which is sanctioned and
honored by the presence,

50:25.520 --> 50:27.920
and power of these two
families around us.

50:30.360 --> 50:32.880
Do you John Michael Shelby

50:32.960 --> 50:35.680
take Esme Martha Lee to
be your beautiful wife?

50:35.760 --> 50:37.240
You got my invitation.

50:37.320 --> 50:40.080
Grace said there's a truce.

50:40.160 --> 50:41.400
Family day.

50:43.800 --> 50:47.200
- Your husband couldn't make it?
- He's not speaking to me.

50:47.280 --> 50:50.280
And when he does,
he calls me, "A fucking Shelby."

50:50.360 --> 50:52.120
Even though I'm a Thorne now.

50:52.200 --> 50:54.240
Thorne in my side that's for sure.

50:54.880 --> 50:57.120
To have and hold--

50:57.200 --> 51:01.080
My God, Tommy.
You admire him, don't you?

51:01.160 --> 51:03.720
There remains one more
part of the ceremony.

51:03.800 --> 51:06.120
That's the mingling of the two bloods.

51:07.600 --> 51:10.440
Where the two families
become the one family.

51:13.320 --> 51:15.240
I now pronounce you
man and wife!

51:15.320 --> 51:16.680
[all cheering]

51:16.760 --> 51:19.200
Come on, John, kiss the bride, will you?

51:19.280 --> 51:21.120
[cheering]

51:27.320 --> 51:29.760
[all singing]

51:58.320 --> 51:59.480
[vomits]

52:01.320 --> 52:02.840
[cheering]

52:10.120 --> 52:12.880
[Ada laughing]

52:17.760 --> 52:19.280
[Ada] Spin me round again.

52:19.360 --> 52:22.760
- Tell Ada to slow down.
- You think she'll listen to me?

52:22.840 --> 52:25.440
I tried to stop her but
she's been drinking.

52:25.520 --> 52:28.120
She's been stuck in that basement
for weeks.

52:28.200 --> 52:31.440
What do you expect?
She's going off like a firecracker.

52:31.520 --> 52:33.040
Oh, Christ, Tommy, please.

52:33.200 --> 52:35.760
[Arthur] Come here.
Enough now. Enough.

52:35.840 --> 52:39.480
Ada, come on, have a rest. Sit down.

52:39.560 --> 52:43.080
Come and look Esme!
Look at the family you've joined!

52:43.160 --> 52:47.240
Come, look at the man who runs it!
He chooses his brother's wives!

52:47.320 --> 52:52.360
He hunts his own sister down like a rat
and tries to kill his own brother-in-law.

52:52.440 --> 52:53.640
Ada, that's enough.

52:53.720 --> 52:56.440
Now he won't even let me have
a fucking dance!

52:56.520 --> 52:59.560
- Not even at a fucking wedding!
- Sit her down.

52:59.640 --> 53:02.000
Calm down, Ada.
Ada, you must calm down.

53:02.080 --> 53:03.520
[panting]

53:03.600 --> 53:06.960
Holy shit. Water. Right.

53:07.640 --> 53:10.600
[Arthur] Bloody hell Ada,
you do pick your times.

53:10.680 --> 53:13.400
[all clamoring]

53:17.080 --> 53:18.560
[car approaches]

53:18.640 --> 53:20.200
[Arthur] Stop! Stop! Stop!

53:20.280 --> 53:22.600
Come here.
Enough now. Enough.

53:22.680 --> 53:24.360
Slow her up.
Nice and easy.

53:24.440 --> 53:26.720
[John] Story of your life, Arthur.

53:26.800 --> 53:27.920
Right, we are here.

53:30.560 --> 53:31.800
Come on.

53:33.360 --> 53:35.640
Nice car, John.
How's she run?

53:35.720 --> 53:37.200
Beautiful. Really smooth.

53:37.280 --> 53:40.640
Your sister's giving birth and
you're talking about a car.

53:40.720 --> 53:42.560
Not much us men can do now.

53:42.640 --> 53:45.040
- Except go get drunk.
- Right, come on.

53:45.120 --> 53:47.200
There's one man who should be here.

53:50.480 --> 53:53.400
You are right, Pol,
Freddie should be here.

53:54.400 --> 53:57.000
Is that a heartbeat
I hear inside that chest?

53:57.080 --> 53:59.000
The truce lasts till sunrise.

53:59.080 --> 54:01.560
On my oath.
Tell Freddie it's safe.

54:01.640 --> 54:02.920
Ada!

54:03.000 --> 54:05.720
Right boys,
let's wet the baby's head.

54:07.160 --> 54:09.280
[loud groaning]

54:09.360 --> 54:11.840
Keep going.
That's right. Push.

54:13.520 --> 54:15.000
[screams]

54:17.480 --> 54:19.840
I think it might be
the wrong way round.

54:19.920 --> 54:20.960
[screams]

54:21.040 --> 54:22.640
I attended three sisters.

54:24.120 --> 54:25.320
You're right.

54:25.400 --> 54:28.280
- We should lean her forward.
- Come on, Ada.

54:28.360 --> 54:32.440
Come on.
Not long to go now, darling.

54:32.520 --> 54:34.080
[screaming]

54:34.160 --> 54:36.800
[Polly] Push, two, three.

54:36.880 --> 54:38.600
[Arthur] To beautiful women.

54:39.840 --> 54:43.360
I should go.
It's my wedding night.

54:43.440 --> 54:44.920
You sit down.

54:45.000 --> 54:48.600
You don't want to be among women when
there's a baby coming.

54:48.680 --> 54:51.360
You think her husband will stay away?

54:51.440 --> 54:56.080
[John] Freddie will be there.
Nothing will keep him away.

54:56.160 --> 55:00.120
Tommy said it's all right
for him to be there. Isn't that right?

55:00.200 --> 55:03.160
That's right.
I'm all heart tonight.

55:03.240 --> 55:05.680
[laughter]

55:05.760 --> 55:09.080
You know what, John boy?

55:10.560 --> 55:15.640
I think it's that lovely barmaid,
that pretty one that just walked out

55:15.720 --> 55:18.640
that's made our brother go all soft.

55:18.720 --> 55:20.040
[John] Drinks to that.

55:21.680 --> 55:23.680
[inaudible]

55:35.840 --> 55:36.920
Ada!

55:41.680 --> 55:43.640
Come on, open up.

55:43.720 --> 55:44.920
[crying]

55:47.360 --> 55:48.920
There you go, love.

55:52.560 --> 55:54.440
Aw, he's beautiful.

55:54.520 --> 55:55.640
[door opens]

55:59.320 --> 56:00.800
It's a boy, Freddie.

56:07.720 --> 56:09.960
It's a beautiful baby boy.

56:20.920 --> 56:22.080
There you go.

56:23.720 --> 56:25.360
Welcome to the world, son.

56:27.720 --> 56:28.840
Welcome.

56:28.920 --> 56:30.920
Open up! Police!

56:31.000 --> 56:32.640
[banging on door]

56:32.720 --> 56:34.240
Freddie...

56:34.320 --> 56:38.000
You can't come in here.
There's a baby just been born.

56:39.920 --> 56:42.240
[Ada] Don't hurt him. Freddie.

56:42.320 --> 56:44.560
You are hurting him,
leave him alone.

56:44.640 --> 56:47.280
You're taking me away from my baby.

56:47.360 --> 56:50.000
- Ada!
- Freddie!

56:59.200 --> 57:01.360
[John and Arthur singing]

57:05.320 --> 57:07.920
You want me to open
that champagne now?

57:08.000 --> 57:09.160
[door slams]

57:10.080 --> 57:11.200
It's a boy.

57:13.600 --> 57:15.680
Pol? Polly?
Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!

57:15.760 --> 57:17.480
Polly! Polly!

57:18.080 --> 57:20.800
But the police came and
took his father away.

57:20.880 --> 57:23.200
Don't you dare look at me like that.

57:32.360 --> 57:33.840
You liar!

57:45.720 --> 57:49.720
[theme song playing]
